  "worked_solution": "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
